###Pure

// core-fx -- FX rates keyed by pair and date, plus the conversion arithmetic that
// downstream projects (valuation-core and everything above it) call rather than
// re-implement.
//
// The one idea worth stating up front, because every class below encodes it: an FX rate
// is NOT keyed by the pair alone, and not even by pair and date. On a single cob date the
// same pair has a SPOT rate, a set of FORWARD outrights and an official FIXING, and they
// are different numbers -- a 4pm WM/R fixing is not the 17:00 close spot, and a 3M
// forward is spot plus points. So the key is (pair, cob date, rate type), which is the
// composite primary key this project exists to exercise.

// What a currency pair IS in the market, as opposed to two currency codes in some order.
// The market quotes EURUSD, never USDEUR, and the rate means "quote currency per ONE unit
// of base currency" -- so EURUSD 1.0850 is 1.0850 USD per EUR.
//
// `quotedInverted` records which side of that convention USD lands on, because it decides
// whether a downstream valuation multiplies or divides:
//
//   EURUSD, GBPUSD   USD is the QUOTE currency. A EUR amount * rate is already USD.
//                    quotedInverted = false.
//   USDJPY, USDCHF   USD is the BASE currency. A JPY amount must be divided by the rate
//                    (or multiplied by core_fx::invert of it) to reach USD.
//                    quotedInverted = true.
//
// Getting this backwards is the classic FX bug: it does not throw, it just returns a
// number roughly 1/x^2 away from the right one and looks plausible on USDCHF.
Class core_fx::CfxCurrencyPair
{
   // The six-character market code, e.g. EURUSD. Base then quote, always in the order
   // the market actually quotes it.
   pairCode: String[1];
   // ISO 4217 of the currency ONE unit of which the rate prices, e.g. EUR in EURUSD.
   baseCurrency: String[1];
   // ISO 4217 of the currency the rate is expressed in, e.g. USD in EURUSD.
   quoteCurrency: String[1];
   // True when the market quotes USD as the BASE (USDJPY, USDCHF), so valuing a
   // foreign-currency amount in USD needs core_fx::invert first.
   quotedInverted: Boolean[1];
   // Pips per unit of the quote currency: 10000 for EURUSD/GBPUSD/USDCHF, 100 for the
   // JPY pairs. Forward points are quoted in pips, so this is what turns them into rate.
   pipFactor: Float[1];
   // Decimal places a quote is published to: 5 for EURUSD, 3 for USDJPY.
   quotePrecision: Integer[1];
   // True for the G10 majors -- EURUSD, USDJPY, GBPUSD, USDCHF all are.
   isMajor: Boolean[1];
   // Reads the convention out loud, for a report header that should not have to know it.
   convention() { $this.quoteCurrency + ' per 1 ' + $this.baseCurrency } : String[1];
}

// One published rate for one pair, on one cob date, of one type -- the grain of this
// project and the row a downstream valuation looks up. Identity is the triple
// (pairCode, cobDate, rateType); dropping any of the three collapses distinct market
// observations onto each other.
//
// rateType is one of SPOT, FORWARD, FIXING:
//   SPOT     the T+2 rate at the close snap
//   FORWARD  an outright for a tenor -- spot plus forwardPoints / pipFactor
//   FIXING   the official published benchmark (WM/R 16:00 London, ECB 14:15 CET)
Class core_fx::CfxRate
{
   // Part 1 of the key: which pair, in market convention order.
   pairCode: String[1];
   // Part 2 of the key: the close-of-business date the rate belongs to.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // Part 3 of the key: SPOT, FORWARD or FIXING. Same pair, same day, three numbers.
   rateType: String[1];
   // Bid: what the market pays for the base currency.
   bid: Float[1];
   // Ask: what the market sells the base currency for. Always >= bid.
   ask: Float[1];
   // Mid: the number carried into valuation when no side is specified.
   mid: Float[1];
   // Present only on FORWARD rows: 1W, 1M, 3M, 6M, 1Y.
   tenor: String[0..1];
   // Present only on FORWARD rows: the outright's premium over spot, quoted in PIPS,
   // so it must be divided by pipFactor before being added to spot.
   forwardPoints: Float[0..1];
   // Which publisher this came from -- joins to CfxFixingSource.
   sourceCode: String[1];
   // True when this is the golden rate the firm books against, rather than an indication.
   isOfficial: Boolean[1];
   // The dealing spread in rate terms, for a data-quality screen that should not have to
   // re-derive it.
   spread() { $this.ask - $this.bid } : Float[1];
}

// Where a rate came from, and at what instant. Two firms disagreeing on "the" EURUSD rate
// for a date are almost always reading different sources, so the source is part of the
// answer and not a footnote.
Class core_fx::CfxFixingSource
{
   // Short code carried on every rate row, e.g. WMR, ECB, CME.
   sourceCode: String[1];
   // Human name, e.g. 'WM/Refinitiv Closing Spot Rate'.
   sourceName: String[1];
   // The snap instant as published, e.g. '16:00' for WM/R, '14:15' for the ECB.
   fixingTime: String[1];
   // The zone that instant is stated in -- 'Europe/London' for WM/R, 'Europe/Berlin' for
   // the ECB. Without it the fixing time is ambiguous twice a year.
   timeZone: String[1];
   // True when the source is an authorised benchmark administrator under EU BMR.
   isRegulated: Boolean[1];
}

// A cross rate that is not quoted directly and must be triangulated through a vehicle
// currency. EURJPY has no primary market quote; it is EURUSD * USDJPY, USD being the
// vehicle. This class records which two legs a given cross is built from, so the
// derivation is data rather than a hard-coded rule in each downstream project.
Class core_fx::CfxCrossRate
{
   // The cross being built, e.g. EURJPY.
   crossCode: String[1];
   // The cob date the two legs are taken from -- both legs must be the same date.
   cobDate: StrictDate[1];
   // The currency triangulated through, USD for essentially every G10 cross.
   vehicleCurrency: String[1];
   // First leg, e.g. EURUSD. Its quote currency is the vehicle.
   legOneCode: String[1];
   // Second leg, e.g. USDJPY. Its base currency is the vehicle.
   legTwoCode: String[1];
   // The triangulated mid, i.e. core_fx::cross(legOneMid, legTwoMid).
   crossMid: Float[1];
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ exported functions
// These are the reason core-fx is a dependency rather than a table. Downstream projects
// call them by path; the signatures below are the contract.

// Value an amount in the rate's quote currency. THE conversion function -- valuation-core
// calls this from a derived property, and everything above it inherits the result.
// Direction is the caller's problem: pass a rate already pointing the way you want, using
// core_fx::invert if the pair is quotedInverted.
function core_fx::convert(amount: Float[1], rate: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   $amount * $rate
}

// Flip a quote to the other direction: USDJPY 157.20 becomes JPY-per-USD's reciprocal,
// 0.00636..., which is what a JPY amount must be multiplied by to reach USD. Needed for
// exactly the pairs where quotedInverted is true.
function core_fx::invert(rate: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   1.0 / $rate
}

// Triangulate a cross through a vehicle currency: EURJPY = EURUSD * USDJPY. Requires the
// first leg's quote currency and the second leg's base currency to both be the vehicle,
// which is what CfxCrossRate records.
function core_fx::cross(legOne: Float[1], legTwo: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   $legOne * $legTwo
}

// Build a forward outright from spot and forward points. Points are quoted in pips, so
// they are scaled by the pair's pipFactor -- 10000 for EURUSD, 100 for USDJPY. Passing
// the wrong factor is off by 100x and is why pipFactor is on the pair and not assumed.
function core_fx::forwardOutright(spot: Float[1], forwardPoints: Float[1], pipFactor: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   $spot + ($forwardPoints / $pipFactor)
}

// Convert while respecting the pair's quoting convention, so the caller does not have to
// remember which of the four majors puts USD on the base side. This is convert() and
// invert() composed, and is the safe entry point for anything valuing into USD.
function core_fx::convertQuoted(amount: Float[1], rate: Float[1], quotedInverted: Boolean[1]): Float[1]
{
   if($quotedInverted,
      | core_fx::convert($amount, core_fx::invert($rate)),
      | core_fx::convert($amount, $rate))
}

// The mid of a two-way price. Trivial, and worth exporting anyway: every downstream
// project otherwise writes ($bid + $ask) / 2.0 itself and one of them writes it wrong.
function core_fx::midOf(bid: Float[1], ask: Float[1]): Float[1]
{
   ($bid + $ask) / 2.0
}
